{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the heavens and the earth were finished, and all their host.</b> Scripture attached ויכלו to the sixth day, to allude by its initial letters (ר״ת) to the distinctive Name; and to indicate that even though in all the works of Creation He was not mentioned, it is He who makes everything, He who begins and He who completes. And the reason He did not say וכל צבאם in the first verse is that there He mentioned the sign of the accusative (את), which indicates plurality for all the hosts; but here, since He did not mention את, it was necessary to say וכל צבאם. And the word צבאם includes the angels, in the manner of what is written (I Kings 22:19): ״וכל צבא השמים״ and so forth; and Scripture says (Isaiah 24:21): ״יפקד ה׳ על צבא המרום במרום״ — HaShem will punish the host of the height in the height — and afterward: ״ועל מלכי האדמה באדמה״ — and upon the kings of the earth on the earth; for no nation falls until its ministering prince falls first. And it includes the souls as well, yet no act of creation is mentioned for them in all the works of Genesis — neither for the angels nor for the souls. And the reason He said וכל צבאם in the singular and not צבאותם is to indicate that the heavens and the earth are all unified forces, bound one to the other, for all of existence is three parts: the world of angels, the world of the spheres, and the lower world. The world of angels is the soul for the spheres, and the spheres act upon the lower beings — if so, everything is bound and unified one with the other. And a full verse testifies to this, as it is written (Hosea 2:23): ״אענה נאם ה׳ אענה את השמים והם יענו את הארץ״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:16.132210089Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}